---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<Base
  title="A Short Winning Rule for the Square Achievement Game"
  description="A first-player strategy for the square achievement game on every board from 6 by 6 up, stated as a rule with no lookup table, verified by exhaustive search and formalized in Lean. Play against it and watch it explain its moves."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>A Short Winning Rule for the Square Achievement Game</h1>
    <p class="byline">Sean Reid · September 2026</p>
  </header>

  <p class="lead">
    The winning strategy of the threshold result consulted a table of some eight
    thousand forcing plans. This result replaces the table by a rule: open at
    (2,2), answer the first reply with a fixed second move, take a completion or
    a block whenever one exists, and otherwise follow the shortest line of at
    most three single forcing moves ending in the arms of a <em>plus</em>, a
    held stone with the four cells at a common distance along the axes. The rule
    wins every board from 6 × 6 up, by the seventeenth ply at the latest.
  </p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem.</span> For every
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 6")} />, the first player following the plus rule
    wins the square achievement game on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board.
  </div>

  <p>
    A plus of side <span set:html={k("s")} /> is a centre with four arms at distance
    <span set:html={k("s")} /> up, down, left, and right. Once the centre and one
    arm are held, each further arm perpendicular to a held arm makes three corners
    of a square whose fourth corner is a diagonal cell, so the opponent must block
    there; the last arm is perpendicular to two held arms and threatens two squares
    at once. When no plus can be completed directly, the rule first plays tempo moves,
    single forcing moves that each add a stone the opponent must answer. The rule
    reads only its own stones, the opponent's stones inside a 17 × 17 region, and
    one derived cell, so the same rule serves every board, and it plays the same 79
    lines on every board from 6 × 6 to 17 × 17.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="6" strategy="plus"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Play against the rule. The bot moves first, explains each move, and draws
      the line it is following. Change n to explore.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <nav class="routes" aria-label="where to go next">
    <a class="route" href={`${base}downloads/plus.pdf`}>
      <span class="route-title">Read the paper</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The rule in full, why plus arms force, how the rule was found in the
        table, the verification runs, and the formal theorem that the rule's own
        play wins. PDF, built from this repository.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a class="route" href={`${base}plus/proof/`}>
      <span class="route-title">See how the proof works</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        An illustrated guide: pluses and tempo moves, the latent corner, what is
        checked by machine and what by hand, and how one run on the 23 × 23
        board covers every larger board.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="route"
      href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
    >
      <span class="route-title">Check it yourself</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The Lean 4 development. The rule is the definition <code>plusMove</code>
        in <code>Plus/Rule.lean</code>, 250 lines with no data; a successful
        <code>lake build</code> is the proof.
      </span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <h2>What you have to trust</h2>
  <p>
    The headline statement in the development reads as follows.
    <code>WinsWith σ n h</code> says that the game tree in which the first player
    plays the move of the strategy <code>σ</code> at every node after the history
    <code>h</code>, against every legal reply, ends in a first-player square on
    every branch.
  </p>
  <pre
    class="lean"><code>theorem plus_wins_with {"{"}n : ℕ{"}"} (h : 6 ≤ n) :
    WinsWith plusMove n []</code></pre>
  <p>
    This is a statement about the rule's own play, not only that a winning tree
    exists. The definitions behind it, cells, squares, the winning predicate,
    and the 250 lines of <code>plusMove</code>, are the part a skeptic has to
    read. Beyond them the trust is Lean's kernel, mathlib, and
    <code>native_decide</code>, which lets the Lean compiler evaluate the six
    exhaustive runs instead of the kernel. The bot on this page is a TypeScript
    copy of the rule, checked against the Lean definition on every reply pair of
    the 6 × 6 board and on the anchored positions of the proof.
  </p>
</Base>
